MySQL Triggers

A trigger is automatic SQL logic/an event that MySQL executes Before/After, when a specific INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE event happens on a table
You do not call a trigger manually - The table event causes it to run automatically
-> MySQL triggers are row-level: they execute once for each affected row

Application
    |
INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE
    |
Table changes
    |
TRIGGER automatically fires
    |
Extra database action

1. Real-Life Example First
Imagine a Zepto/Instamart-style system

You have:
orders
inventory
order_audit

-- A customer places an order
Your application executes: INSERT INTO orders (...);
But the business also wants: an audit record
                            inventory adjustment
                            automatic timestamp
                            validation
                            status tracking
Instead of trusting every application developer to remember those extra operations, 
some of this logic can be enforced at the database layer with triggers

Trigger example:
New order inserted
        |
AFTER INSERT trigger
        |
Create audit record

2. Trigger vs Stored Procedure

-- Stored Procedure
You explicitly run: CALL place_order(...);

-- Trigger
You run: INSERT INTO orders (...);
and MySQL automatically fires the trigger

Procedure = You call it
Trigger = An event causes it to run

-- Standard Trigger Syntax
CREATE TRIGGER trigger_name
BEFORE | AFTER
INSERT | UPDATE | DELETE
ON table_name
FOR EACH ROW
trigger_body;
--> MySQL supports BEFORE and AFTER timing with INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE, and every MySQL trigger is FOR EACH ROW

